This makes a bit more sense now that I slept on it. In a bifactor model there are more loadings but the number of intercepts depends on the number of items. There is one intercept for each item, just like there is one intercept when we have one outcome and many predictors in a regression.

In this case, I have 65 items, so I guess the degrees of freedom increase only by 50 because there are 15 marker variables (one for each factor)?

