> > behavior for WLS when there are both ordinal (categorical) and
> > continuous variables.  In this case, using the full WLS produces
> > positively biased estimates, but the estimates from diagonally WLS or
> > ULS are fine.
>
> Hello Michael.
>
> Four months later, I (re)discover this interesting post. (In August, I saw your post, thought it was interesting, and then -in true vacation modus- forgot all about it.)
>
> There is indeed bias when using WLS for your model/data combination, but I think this is not due to a 'bug' in the software (or the methodology). Reassuringly, Mplus gives exactly the same results as lavaan. For the skeptics, I attach the dataset (mplus.raw, N=10000), and the mplus input/output files, as well as the the lavaan output in lavaan_output.txt).
>
> (As to why the numbers between OpenMx and lavaan are slightly different, this is mostly a matter of how exactly the weight matrix is computed. Different approaches are possible, and lavaan and OpenMx use slightly different strategies. I do not think this matters much.)
>
> As was correctly pointed out by Stas, the reason for the 'bias' in this case is model misspecification! WLS is much less forgiving if the model is misspecified, compared to DWLS/ULS and even ML. When the model is closer to the data, the bias goes away. For example:
