Yves,

I am trying to evaluate the likelihood of a model with new data following your post of Nov 3, 2014.  As a check, I ran the logliklihood estimation using the original data.  The loglikelhood calculated using dmvnorm and the loglikelihood returned by fitmeasures differ by more than 2. When I try the exact same code on your example, it works fine.  I am at a loss as to why the calculation doesn't work on my object.  I am  hoping you can point out some stupid error on my part.

Thanks very much, Mark Taper

> fit=tst$theta

> Y <- fit@Data@X[[1]] # data as a matrix
> Mu.hat <- fitted(fit)$mean
> Sigma.hat <- fitted(fit)$cov
>
> # loglikelihood
> sum(mvtnorm:::dmvnorm(x=Y, mean=Mu.hat, sigma=Sigma.hat, log=TRUE))

[1] -9874.424
> fitmeasures(fit,fit.measures="logl")
     logl
-9872.178

On Monday, November 3, 2014 at 10:17:19 AM UTC-7, yrosseel wrote:
> On 10/31/2014 03:46 PM, John Prindle wrote:
> > model with no free parameters to get a likelihood for data2.
>
> If you only need the loglikelihood, you can easily compute this 'by
> hand'. Assuming multivariate normality, all we need are the
> model-implied mean and covariance matrix.
>
> For example:
>
> library(lavaan)
> HS.model <- ' visual  =~ x1 + x2 + x3
>                textual =~ x4 + x5 + x6
>                speed   =~ x7 + x8 + x9 '
> fit <- cfa(HS.model, data=HolzingerSwineford1939, meanstructure = TRUE)
>
> Y <- fit@Data@X[[1]] # data as a matrix
> Mu.hat <- fitted(fit)$mean
> Sigma.hat <- fitted(fit)$cov
>
> # loglikelihood
> sum(mvtnorm:::dmvnorm(x=Y, mean=Mu.hat, sigma=Sigma.hat, log=TRUE))
>
> # or, using the sample moments only:
> n <- nobs(fit)
> S <- inspect(fit, "sampstat")$cov
> m <- inspect(fit, "sampstat")$mean
> nvar <- length(m)
>
> -n*nvar/2*log(2*pi) - n/2*log(det(Sigma.hat)) -
> n/2*sum(diag(solve(Sigma.hat) %*% (S + tcrossprod(m-Mu.hat))))
>
>
> of course, you can replace 'Y' or the sample moments 'S' and 'm' with
> new data.
>
>
> Yves.

